    <DataNode elementId="AT2G14920-MONOMER" textLabel="brassinosteroid sulfotransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O82330" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="495" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The ST4a protein is a member of the SULT203 family of sulfotransferases |CITS: [15167709]|. Though it was tested against a broad range of substrates including phenolic acids, desulfoglucosinolates and several types of plant hormones, this enzyme specifically acted on brassinosteroids in &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays |CITS: [17039368]|. In particular, ST4a works on active brassinosteroids including castasterone, brassinolide, and (&lt;i&gt;22R&lt;/i&gt;,&lt;i&gt;23R&lt;/i&gt;)-28-homobrassinosteroids but not on related mammalian hydroxysteroids or estrogens |CITS: [17039368]|. The C22-hydroxyl group seems like a likely target for the sulfate addition (as depicted in two reactions for this enzyme), but the exact site of modification has not been identified. Transcript analyses indicate that the ST4a gene is expressed primarily in roots, and that its expression can be repressed by cytokinin, indicating an opportunity for hormone crosstalk |CITS: [17039368]|, but the biological relevance of this finding has not been explored. Two similar proteins, ST4b (At1g13420) and St4c (At1g13430) were also tested but showed no &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; activity with brassinosteroids |CITS: [17039368]|. The biological function of this enzyme has not yet been deduced. Given its relative specificity for active brassinosteroids, it is likely to act in the regulation of this important phytohormone. However, sulfated brassinosteorids have not yet been conclusively detected &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; in plants |CITS: [17039368]|. There is some indirect evidence for their existence |CITS: [YOKOTA92]|, but the biological activity of these compounds remains to be determined |CITS: [17039368]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G14920-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G14920.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O82330" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33687, RXN-16895, RXN-16896, RXN-16899, RXN-16900, RXN-16901, RXN-16902, RXN-16905" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="8" />
